ickly they they certainly can  harry how bad do you think this has the  potential to get because  this is all taking place at a time of  heightened tension but also on the  so-called jerusalem day a day which is  uh  quite provocative for many people  living in the city  well yes that's right i mean even when  there aren't that the current level of  tensions jerusalem day as it's known to  israeli jews  is an extremely uh high-pressure event  uh requiring a lot of israeli security  forces  presence and uh putting on the the  palestinian  residents especially of the muslim  quarter of the old city a great deal of  pressure  as well what it commemorates as far as  israelis are concerned  uh is what they call the unification of  jerusalem  in 1967 what for palestinians who live  here represents the beginning  of the israeli occupation in this half  of the city and of course more widely  across the palestinian territories on  this day  the question is whether the israeli  security forces are going to inhibit  at least some of the route of that march  put in restrictions  to prevent close proximity  between jews and palestinians they would  usually march through the muslim quarter  waving flags dancing chanting shouting  banging on  shut muslim owners shops  and if that takes place today then as  we've just seen  already there have been very violent  events that could trigger  even further such events the israeli  police commissioner said as late as  sunday night  that he wanted things to go ahead as  they traditionally do  however there is meant to be a decision  made early monday  we're still waiting to hear the official  decision as to whether they're going to  allow it to go ahead in its  full form whether they're going to place  restrictions in either event  it'll be tense it uh will indeed harry  falsett they're reporting live for us  at the al-aqsa compound thank you harry  for now  well let's stay in occupied east  jerusalem we can  hear now from es abdel razak an advocacy  director for the palestine institute for  public diplomacy  it's good to have you with us here on  the programme  you've been down at the alexa site  just tell us what you've been seeing  hello enes can you hear us in us abdul  raziq  yes hi there uh yeah i was just telling  our viewers that you've been down at the  sites you've been at the  al-aqsa compound give us a sense of what  you've been seeing when you were there  look um we cannot talk about clashes  between two parties or  tensions this is clearly a repression by  heavy armed  occupation force against unarmed  worshippers uh  and people who are just trying to  protest so this is the situation and as  you can see there is a lot of injured  uh we also saw that uh you know the  paramedics  were prevented to access the site uh  there was a clinic that was attacked  also by stun bombs um so it's uh it's  it's dramatic but it's not happening in  a vacuum  it it it's not at all  do you think then that the the israeli  response to to whatever the israeli  forces were responding to  do you think it was is disproportionate  then  israel's response is always  disproportionate we're talking here  about jerusalemites palestinians who  have been repeatedly  told since generations that this city  is for the israelis and the jewish only  they are born with inferior rights  they are constantly under pressure  repressed oppressed  so sometimes it burst and i think it  won't go away even if tomorrow  it doesn't make the headlines in the  media given the um  you know the emperor it's clearly a  problem that will stay because  palestinians  are not you know are denied their  fundamental rights  in this city and the israelis have  emboldened um  the settlers and they are also  emboldened by  constant impunity well there's a clear  sense of injustice there for  people who are um who are living  in this situation but but just in terms  of the visuals now  you're a palestinian how did it make you  feel  when you saw sun grenades  tear gas being fired inside  the the mosque itself one of the holiest  sites  in islam how did that make you feel  you know angry angry but we're not  surprised  because again this is not the first time  it won't be the last time you can see  that the israeli forces they consider  palestinians the palestinians in front  of them as a  security threat as a democratic as a  demographic threat  not as human beings as citizens with  rights  uh you know we are considered a threat  and  i think this is overall what how  israel's system  uh you know functions whether it's the  legislative the  executive or the armed forces uh this is  a system it's a sophisticated system  it's not only  you know just a few policemen uh these  policemen receive orders  and and they uh have been elected and  and trained  um and elected officials have you know  supported  such uh actions so again this is not  comes as a as a surprise this is  uh decades of of uh um  education mentality and political and  policy practices  given this how how do you think the  international community should be  regarding all of this because whenever  there is unrest of this type  there are usually calls for for for calm  on both sides  here and as you pointed out one side  has rocks and and empty bottles the  other side has  rubber rubber bullets and and tear gas  canisters  how do you think the international  community watching these  images she should be responding to this  i mean by starting by reframing that  narrative  we can't continue to both sides uh you  know what's happening  it's not both sides issue there is an  occupier an occupy there's a colonizer  and a colonized  and unfortunately the international  community has not recognized that and so  they're always  making false equivalences and these  false equivalences prevent  us to move forward towards a just future  so the only  solution now is accountability israel  has never been held accountable for its  continuous violations of international  law  and human rights and let's repeat it  what they're doing  in jerusalem whether it's the forced  expulsions  or what they're doing in al-aqsa is war  crimes and it's it can amount to crimes  against humanity  including the crime of apartheid and  this has been analyzed so  the the response should be  accountability and actions because  uh declarations are not enough well  do you think uh palestinians will ever  get this  accountability do you think this will  ever come  yes i'm hopeful and you know  palestinians again they won't go away  and they won't i think back down they  will continue to mobilize  so i i would urge also the media to  you know continue to look when there is  not this outburst of violence because  often and most often uh the violence  of the occupation and the the colonial  aspects of  where we live is you know is is  underlined is is continuous  and is of low intensity but low  intensity doesn't make it to media  headlines  so i think this is there is a root cause  to all of this we need to address the  root causes  without addressing the root causes uh  this this will continue and palestinians  will continue to be killed  and injured and will continue to  mobilize  okay yes abdul razak there joining us  from occupied east jerusalem  thank you so much for joining us on the  program
 

